Z||000010||87ECO5||10-23-2000||01:55 PM||||Cat. convertor - not torque convertor.<P>There was no 1983 Corvette.  Due to problems, Chevrolet introduced the C4 in 1983 as an '84 model - missing the Vette's 30th anniversary (and pi$$ing off lots and lots of Vette fanatics)!<P>If I'm not mistaken, '73 was the last year to burn "leaded" gasoline so it didn't use a cat.<p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 10-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||FIEROCIOUS||10-23-2000||06:02 PM||||87ECO5 is correct a 73 corvette never came with a catylitic converter. Catylitic converters were not required in california until 74 and the other 49 until 75 Corvette AMC Gremlin same differance matter of fact an AutoZone add was running a year or so saying they can get any part for your car and one of the guys comes up and asks for a Cat for an 73 Gremlin I cought it wonder if anyone else did?<P>But still no answers on the T-5 question? Come on some ones gotta be a rocket scientist and memorize dictionarys?<P>New question:<BR>What size oil filter did a V8 55 Chevy truck use? ||||reg||1
Z||000012||rockcrawl||10-23-2000||06:35 PM||||I think the instructor needs to go back to auto tech school! The air-cooled bettle engine does have a thermostat. It doesn't have a set temperature, it's an adjustable thermostat. It opens air diverters as the engine warms up. I guess that puts me one point ahead of SCCA Fiero and FIEROCIOUS. <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/vwtherm_1.jpg">  <P>My '57 Chevy V8 uses a toilet paper oil filter.<P>Don't know about the Nissan, I'm not into rice!<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 10-23-2000).]||||reg||1
